WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.480
The census is the catalyst of American
public policy.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.340
It shapes voting districts

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.540
and it determines how your public
schools will get funded. Because this

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.550
data is used in myriad democratic and
economic functions it's critical that we

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.690
get it right.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.109
Unfortunately the 2020 edition might be

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.490
in trouble, which has caused concern
among experts for the integrity of the

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.460
country's most authoritative dataset.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.080
The census is centuries old, but this
story starts at the conclusion of the

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:41.940
2010 census. The Bureau had shot past
cost estimates on its way to the most

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.910
expensive census per capita of all time.
So in 1970 it cost $16 to count each

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:54.000
housing unit and in 2010 it cost around
92 dollars to count each housing unit.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
This is all in 2020 dollars. That's
Robert Goldenkoff.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.059
His office redid the

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.180
2020 census on its list of government
initiatives at a high risk of failure.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.080
A key reason? The funding just isn't there.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.939
Congress limited the budget for the 2020

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.100
census to the amount spent on the 2010
census.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.640
The nation is getting more

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.170
complex and less willing to participate
in the census and because the

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:28.110
Constitution requires a complete count,
the Census Bureau has to do more work in

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.580
order to obtain that complete count.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.560
So why might it cost more to count certain groups?

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.360
This scene from an episode of
Master Of None gives some clues.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:41.820
It follows a cab driver back to his
apartment, where he's living with at

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.560
least three other adult men. So this one
apartment contains four distinct

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.940
households in New York City, a region of
notoriously hard to count tracts.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.860
Census tracts are designated "hard to
count" for many reasons.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.460
They could be

00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:02.560
full of renters or they could have an
abundant homeless population.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.979
Topographically complex regions are hard
to count, as are tracts with limited

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.560
access to broadband internet.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.460
It's the people at the margins of society.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.760
More impoverished don't speak the language.
Then we have procedures to try to

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.140
compensate for that. Mr. Prewitt would know
about the challenges in taking a census.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.020
He was director during the year 2000
count. The last 2 to 3 percent of the

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.600
census is very expensive.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.940
Further complicating matters, a new proposal to

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.860
change the census could further increase
costs and effect response rates.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.360
The Department of Justice requested that the
bureau add a question about citizenship

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.620
to the short-form decennial census.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.540
The argument in the letter is that the

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:53.959
Justice Department wants better data on
where citizens are in the country,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.740
in order to enforce the voting rights laws.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.079
The citizenship question already appears

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.410
in the American Community Survey which
collects data annually.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.669
It just goes to a much smaller portion
of the country. I can only do

00:03:07.669 --> 00:03:11.299
consequences and I can say the
consequences of acting as if you need

00:03:11.299 --> 00:03:15.049
this for the Voting Rights Act is
inconsistent with the fact that it

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:16.160
already exists.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.320
A more complete dataset
on citizenship could give the Department

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:24.500
of Justice more granular data to work
with, but that would depend on an

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.300
adequate response rate and the national
political climate might make that more difficult.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.700
Some people have expressed
fear that the government might use

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.120
census data to aid deportations or
otherwise harm the communities.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.800
We have experienced in American history where
being counted in the census did

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:46.000
result in painful consequences for the
Japanese, who were interned.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.200
Americans today have a historically low level of
trust with their government. That fact

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.000
paints an unsettling portrait.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.359
Certainly today's environment, if you told the

00:03:56.359 --> 00:03:59.940
authorities where
a lot of undocumented people live, the

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.910
authorities would I think take advantage
of that piece of information. That's

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:03.740
their job.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:10.950
I think the chances of having anything like a complete census, if we

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:16.160
have the citizenship question is simply
not the case.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.640
There are proponents who favor a
mainstream citizenship question. The

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.630
general argument is that census data are
used to apportion seats in Congress and

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.260
distribute funds, but the Constitution
requires the census to count for all

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.690
persons. That's because the census is a
survey of an economy that includes the

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:38.100
contributions of residents who aren't
citizens. The stakes are whether we care

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.000
about about a representative democracy.
The inequality's, already economic

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.440
inequalities are severe if you duplicate
economic inequalities with political

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.860
inequalities, then you simply have a
different kind of society and it's not

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.620
a democracy.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.700
you

